# Drafted by Gemini (R19 sites), edited. Hazards and kindling searches of the five beacon sites.

~ crypt_study
Narrator: Across the damp limestone wall, a mason has carved a family crest of rising suns intertwined with briar roses.
* [History DC 15] Study the carvings -> crypt_study_ok | crypt_study_plain
* [Religion DC 15] Read the god of the morning in the stone -> crypt_study_ok | crypt_study_plain
* Leave it -> END

~ crypt_study_ok
set mm_crypt_hint
Narrator: Those carved suns and briar roses belong to the house of Sir Marius Renathyr. Their stems point toward the weathered crypt on the eastern edge of the courtyard.
-> END

~ crypt_study_plain
Narrator: The chiselled suns and roses are fine old masonry, though their meaning beneath the lichen remains uncertain to you.
-> END

~ crypt_wrong_a
Narrator: A dry stone basin sits at the foot of the sealed crypt door, stained brown where a tithe of warm blood will unbar the lock.
* Pay the tithe, a little blood -> crypt_open_wrong_a
* Leave it -> END

~ crypt_open_wrong_a
damage 1d12 necrotic
check Dexterity DC 17 -> crypt_bats_ok | crypt_bats_fail

~ crypt_wrong_c
Narrator: Another sealed stone door waits behind a dry offering basin at its threshold, hungry for a fresh drop of blood to release the bolt.
* Pay the tithe, a little blood -> crypt_open_wrong_c
* Leave it -> END

~ crypt_open_wrong_c
damage 1d12 necrotic
check Dexterity DC 17 -> crypt_bats_ok | crypt_bats_fail

~ crypt_bats_ok
Narrator: Leathery wings erupt from the open doorway, and you duck low as the cold draft washes over your necks. Beyond the threshold lies only a hollow, dust-choked vault that feels entirely wrong.
-> crypt_empty

~ crypt_bats_fail
damage 4d4 piercing
Narrator: A screaming cloud of bats pours from the dark doorway, tearing at your faces with needle teeth and hooked claws.
-> crypt_empty

~ crypt_empty
if flag.mm_crimson_fails < 2
set mm_crimson_fails += 1
endif
yorgrim [neutral]: This vault holds only dust, not the knight we seek. Keep your blades drawn, for the racket has woken something out in the fog.
-> END

~ crypt_right
Narrator: A dry blood basin rests at the foot of this sealed door, crowned by the very same suns and briar roses carved upon the courtyard wall.
if flag.mm_crypt_hint
Narrator: Here stands the eastern crypt that the carved roses promised.
endif
* Pay the tithe, a little blood -> crypt_open_right
* Leave it -> END

~ crypt_open_right
damage 1d12 necrotic
check Dexterity DC 17 -> crypt_right_ok | crypt_right_fail

~ crypt_right_ok
Narrator: Bats burst from the unsealed arch as you duck beneath their frantic wings, leaving the stone stair into the dark wide open.
set mm_crypt_open
move mm_highborn_crypts:below_from_stair

~ crypt_right_fail
damage 4d4 piercing
Narrator: A shrieking swarm of bats explodes from the unsealed arch, biting at your bare skin as the stone stair yawns open below.
set mm_crypt_open
move mm_highborn_crypts:below_from_stair

~ rose_take
give mm_dried_rose
set mm_rose_taken
Narrator: Upon the cold sarcophagus lid rests a dried red rose as weightless as ash paper, and you slip its brittle stem into your pack.
-> END

~ fountain
Narrator: Cold rain patters into a mossy wishing fountain where tarnished silver and copper coins gleam beneath the bubbling dark water.
* Toss in a gold piece for luck -> fountain_toss
* Take the coins -> fountain_take
* [Arcana DC 18] Work out what the fountain does -> fountain_study | fountain_plain
* Leave it -> END

~ fountain_study
Narrator: Old churchyard custom clings to the cold basin: a coin dropped in grants a modest blessing, while a coin fished out invites a spiteful curse. Either mark will fade before a few hours pass.
set mm_fountain_known
-> fountain

~ fountain_plain
Narrator: The dark water burbles over the old coins and keeps its secret.
-> fountain

~ fountain_toss
gold -1
bless party 2h
Narrator: The coin sinks through the black water with a soft plink, and a quiet warmth like hearth-smoke settles on your shoulders.
-> END

~ fountain_take
check Wisdom DC 18 -> fountain_take_ok | fountain_take_cursed

~ fountain_take_ok
gold +300
Narrator: You scoop a handful of wet copper into your pocket, and the still dark water does not stir.
-> END

~ fountain_take_cursed
gold +300
disadvantage checks 2h: the fountain's curse
Narrator: The wet coins sit like river-ice in your palm, and a sour, sinking cold knots in the pit of your stomach.
-> END

~ urn_study
Narrator: Rows of damp clay urns line the mossy walk, and several of them hum a thin, reed-like note that sets your teeth on edge.
* [Nature DC 15] Listen to which hum and which do not -> urn_study_ok | urn_study_plain
* [Religion DC 15] Judge them by their carving -> urn_study_ok | urn_study_plain
* Leave it -> END

~ urn_study_ok
set mm_urn_hint
Narrator: These humming vessels are mourning urns, bound to wail if their seals are broken. Only one pot sits quiet at the end of the row, painted with pale barn owls.
-> END

~ urn_study_plain
Narrator: The thin, droning hum vibrates through the soles of your boots, though you cannot tell what stirs inside the clay.
-> END

~ urn_wrong_a
Narrator: The clay urn vibrates under your fingertips, its mournful hum rising to a sharp pitch as your hand nears the waxed lid.
if flag.mm_urn_hint
Narrator: That is a mourning urn, and breaking its seal will loose a bitter wail.
endif
* Open it anyway -> urn_wail
* Leave it -> END

~ urn_wrong_c
Narrator: The low drone inside the vessel climbs to a shrill whine as your fingers brush the cold clay rim.
* Open it anyway -> urn_wail
* Leave it -> END

~ urn_wail
check Wisdom DC 13 -> urn_wail_ok | urn_wail_fail

~ urn_wail_ok
Narrator: A reed-thin shriek pours from the open mouth of the urn, washing past your ears like a cold wave before fading into the fog.
-> urn_empty

~ urn_wail_fail
damage 4d6 psychic party
Narrator: The keening wail passes right through your ribs like an icy hand through water, leaving the whole party staggering on the wet stones.
-> urn_empty

~ urn_empty
if flag.mm_violet_fails < 2
set mm_violet_fails += 1
endif
yorgrim [neutral]: Leave that clay alone, travelers. The loud ones hold only grey ash and old grief.
-> END

~ urn_right
Narrator: The urn painted with pale barn owls sits cool and silent beneath your hand.
* Open it -> urn_right_open
* Leave it -> END

~ urn_right_open
give mm_owl_feather
set mm_owl_taken
Narrator: Nestled in the dry dust at the bottom of the pot lies a single barn owl feather, white as a new moon.
-> END

~ lavender
give mm_lavender
Narrator: A tangled bed of wild lavender grows against the iron fence, and you snap off a fistful of the sharp, sweet-smelling stems.
-> END

~ lilies
give mm_lilies
Narrator: Rain-heavy white lilies nod in the ditch, and you gather a few cold, waxy blooms by their wet stems.
-> END

~ amber_find
Narrator: Pale amber lights bob through the wet heather like stray sparks, leading your eyes in circles while the iron brazier hides somewhere in the mist.
* [Perception DC 18] Read where the lights gather -> amber_found | amber_lost
* [Survival DC 18] Read the ground between the posts -> amber_found | amber_lost
* Ask Yorgrim to find it -> amber_yorgrim
* Leave it -> END

~ amber_found
set mm_amber_found
Narrator: The drifting amber lights wheel around a muddy hollow, where a dark iron brazier sits heaped with cold orange coals.
-> END

~ amber_lost
time +60
check Dexterity DC 17 -> amber_pit_ok | amber_pit_fail

~ amber_pit_ok
set mm_amber_found
Narrator: You tramp for a bleak hour through the mire,skirts brushing the crumbled lip of an open mass grave without slipping in. At last, the pale beam of the hound's lantern cuts the fog and catches the iron ribs of the brazier.
-> END

~ amber_pit_fail
set mm_amber_found
damage 2d6 necrotic party
Narrator: The rotten sod gives way beneath your boots and drops you into a pit where dry, skeletal fingers claw at your cloaks until you haul yourselves out of the mud. Ahead in the mist, the pale lantern finally gleams against the dark iron of the brazier.
-> END

~ amber_yorgrim
set mm_amber_found
Narrator: The old orc lifts the hooded lantern high, and the false amber lights scatter like moths to leave the iron brazier plain in the gloom.
-> END

~ mass_grave
Narrator: The sodden earth drops away into a yawning trench, where pale bone fingers twitch and scrape in the black mud at the bottom.
-> END

~ grave_study
Narrator: A murder of wet-feathered crows huddles shoulder to shoulder atop a single crooked fencepost.
* [Animal Handling DC 17] Watch where the crows gather -> grave_study_ok | grave_study_plain
* [Perception DC 17] Look for the post they will not leave -> grave_study_ok | grave_study_plain
* Leave it -> END

~ grave_study_ok
set mm_grave_hint
Narrator: Every black beak points east toward one sunken mound in the nettles. If any grave holds dry straw, it is that one.
-> END

~ grave_study_plain
Narrator: The wet crows only cock their heads and rasp at you from the rotting post, offering no sign at all.
-> END

~ grave_wrong_a
Narrator: No sooner does your spade bite into the wet turf than the mound bursts open in a shrieking cloud of black carrion birds.
check Constitution DC 15 -> grave_birds_ok | grave_birds_fail

~ grave_wrong_c
Narrator: Your shovel strikes the shallow earth, and a tangle of greasy black wings erupts from the soil into your faces.
check Constitution DC 15 -> grave_birds_ok | grave_birds_fail

~ grave_birds_ok
Narrator: You duck as the frantic wings beat past your ears, leaving naught in the shallow pit but a few cracked ribs of bone.
-> grave_empty

~ grave_birds_fail
damage 1d8 poison party
Narrator: The beating wings choke the air with sour grave-dust, leaving you bent double and coughing grit from your lungs.
-> grave_empty

~ grave_empty
if flag.mm_amber_fails < 2
set mm_amber_fails += 1
endif
yorgrim [neutral]: There is no straw under that sod, only cold mud. Keep your blades ready, for something pale is stirring in the mist.
-> END

~ grave_right
Narrator: The black crows crowd thickest around the head of this sunken mound.
* Dig -> grave_right_dig
* Leave it -> END

~ grave_right_dig
give mm_straw_bundle
set mm_straw_taken
Narrator: Just beneath the wet roots lies a dry bundle of golden straw, neatly bound with a faded scrap of blue yarn.
-> END

~ muck
set mm_magenta_muck_done
Narrator: Black, sour muck oozes up between the cracked flagstones of the lane, stinging your nostrils with the reek of old rot.
check Strength DC 17 -> muck_ok | muck_fail

~ muck_ok
Narrator: You step from one high stone to the next, keeping your boot-heels clear of the slick black ooze.
-> END

~ muck_fail
damage 1d10 acid
Narrator: A boot punches through the rotten tile into sour, bubbling mud, and the caustic slime scalds flesh until you haul the leg free.
-> END

~ vault_study
Narrator: The iron vault doors bear weathered carvings of tall ships and coiling spirals, with pale hex glyphs etched into their damp stone corners.
* [Arcana DC 17] Read the glyphs -> vault_study_ok | vault_study_plain
* [History DC 17] Read the ships -> vault_study_ok | vault_study_plain
* Leave it -> END

~ vault_study_ok
set mm_vault_hint
Narrator: Down in the south-east lane, one door marries a three-masted ship with a sorcerer's spiral. That will be Captain Briggsy's berth.
-> END

~ vault_study_plain
Narrator: The salt-worn carvings blur together and tell you nothing useful.
-> END

~ vault_wrong_a
Narrator: A heavy vault door blocks the niche, a crooked hex glyph smoldering faintly at its top corner.
* [Athletics DC 20] Force it -> vault_open_wrong | vault_hexed
* Open it anyway and take the hex -> vault_hexed
* Leave it -> END

~ vault_wrong_c
Narrator: Another iron vault door stands shut before you, its corner marked with a dimly glowing hex glyph.
* [Athletics DC 20] Force it -> vault_open_wrong | vault_hexed
* Open it anyway and take the hex -> vault_hexed
* Leave it -> END

~ vault_hexed
damage 2d10 force
disadvantage checks 1h: the vault's hex
-> vault_open_wrong

~ vault_open_wrong
Narrator: The rusted door swings wide on a shelf of yellowed ribs and cracked skulls that never belonged to Captain Briggsy.
if flag.mm_magenta_fails < 2
set mm_magenta_fails += 1
endif
yorgrim [neutral]: Not that berth, friends, those bones are quiet. Something heavy is dragging its chains above the lanes.
-> END

~ vault_right
Narrator: Side by side in the damp stone, a carved brigantine and a tight spiral mark this hexed vault door.
* [Athletics DC 20] Force it -> vault_right_open | vault_right_hex
* Open it anyway and take the hex -> vault_right_hex
* Leave it -> END

~ vault_right_hex
damage 2d10 force
disadvantage checks 1h: the vault's hex
-> vault_right_open

~ vault_right_open
give mm_rum_bottle
set mm_rum_taken
Narrator: Inside on a bone-dry stone ledge sits a dark bottle of spiced rum, its wax-sealed cork still whole.
-> END

~ moth_vault
give mm_moth_wing
set mm_moth_taken
Narrator: Behind the grate rests a tarnished glass case holding a dried moth wing wide as a calloused palm, its dusty eyespots promising deep and dreamless sleep.
-> END

~ emerald_find
Narrator: The mossy barrows seem to trade places in the fog while every muddy track loops back on itself, hiding the green brazier somewhere in the tangle.
* [Survival DC 18] Find the way through the mounds -> emerald_found | emerald_lost
* [Nature DC 18] Read where the trees lean -> emerald_found | emerald_lost
* Ask Yorgrim to find it -> emerald_yorgrim
* Leave it -> END

~ emerald_found
set mm_emerald_found
Narrator: Every crooked bough in the thicket leans toward a single humped mound, where emerald coals smolder in an iron basket.
-> END

~ emerald_lost
time +60
set mm_emerald_ring_ready
Narrator: An hour slips away in the shifting brambles before your boots come to a halt inside a pale ring of spotted toadstools.
-> toadstool_ring

~ toadstool_ring
set mm_ring_done
check Constitution DC 17 -> ring_ok | ring_fail

~ ring_ok
set mm_emerald_found
Narrator: Yellow spores sting your eyes like woodsmoke, though a sweep of the hound's hooded lantern soon cuts the fog to reveal the green brazier.
-> END

~ ring_fail
set mm_emerald_found
damage 2d8 poison party
Narrator: Clouds of bitter toadstool dust sear your lungs and leave you coughing until the hound's lantern light catches the iron brazier ahead.
-> END

~ emerald_yorgrim
set mm_emerald_found
Narrator: The old orc lifts the hooded lantern high, and the grave-mist curls back like wet wool from the cold green coals of the brazier.
-> END

~ apple_tree
Narrator: Wax-red apples dangle from gnarled boughs that curl like grasping fingers.
* [Nature DC 15] Study the apples -> apple_study_ok | apple_study_plain
* Pick one -> apple_pick
* Eat one -> apple_eat
* Leave it -> END

~ apple_study_ok
Narrator: Those cellar-sweet apples carry old wood-magic for weal or woe, and one in twenty will rot the gut where you stand. Worse, the crooked boughs strike like a club at any hand that steals from them.
-> apple_tree

~ apple_study_plain
Narrator: The red skins gleam with cold dew and keep their secrets to themselves.
-> apple_tree

~ apple_pick
check Dexterity DC 15 -> apple_pick_ok | apple_pick_lash

~ apple_pick_ok
give forbidden_apple
Narrator: A knotted limb snaps down like a switch, though you twist a waxy apple from the twig and duck beneath the blow.
-> END

~ apple_pick_lash
damage 1d12 bludgeoning
give forbidden_apple
Narrator: The moment fingers close around the stem, a heavy branch whips sideways with a crack of bark and knocks the picker flat into the mud.
-> END

~ apple_eat
check Constitution DC 10 -> apple_good | apple_bad

~ apple_good
heal 4d4+4 party
Narrator: The crisp flesh tastes of cider and late sun, sending a steady hearth-warmth right down to your numb toes.
-> END

~ apple_bad
damage 4d6 poison
Narrator: The pulp turns to foul ashes on the tongue, and a cold cramp twists your stomach inside out.
-> END

~ mound_study
Narrator: A split yew tree leans over the path, a weeping woman's face and wide stag antlers worn deep into its gray bark.
* [Perception DC 17] Follow the tree's gaze -> mound_study_ok | mound_study_plain
* [Survival DC 17] Follow the roots -> mound_study_ok | mound_study_plain
* Leave it -> END

~ mound_study_ok
set mm_mound_hint
Narrator: Her wooden eyes stare straight east toward the low barrow where a crooked oak bough hangs over the entrance.
-> END

~ mound_study_plain
Narrator: The mossy face in the bark stares out blindly and gives away no secrets.
-> END

~ mound_wrong_a
Narrator: You spade through the wet clay packing the barrow mouth for a long minute, uncovering nothing more than a circle of river stones.
-> mound_empty

~ mound_wrong_c
Narrator: Your blades and boots scrape away the heavy turf at the entrance, revealing only a cold ring of fieldstones in the dirt.
-> mound_empty

~ mound_empty
if flag.mm_emerald_fails < 2
set mm_emerald_fails += 1
endif
yorgrim [neutral]: Leave that dirt be, Farryn does not rest under those stones. The old roots are waking beneath your boots.
-> END

~ mound_right
Narrator: A single crooked oak branch reaches out to shelter the dark mouth of this earthen barrow.
* Dig -> mound_right_dig
* Leave it -> END

~ mound_right_dig
give mm_oak_branch
set mm_oak_taken
Narrator: Beneath a foot of packed grave-loam lies a brittle oak branch with dried leaves still clinging to the twig, laid flat like a mourner's gift.
-> END

~ nightshade
give mm_nightshade
set mm_nightshade_taken
Narrator: Clusters of glossy purple nightshade berries nod in the ditch, and you pinch a toxic sprig free without bruising the stems.
-> END

~ carve_tree
set mm_greenwood_carved
Narrator: Your blade scores the letters of Greenwood deep into the wet bark, and a long shudder runs up the trunk into the leafless crown.
-> END

~ clan_study
Narrator: The weathered markers are chiselled with jagged runes that tug at the edge of your memory, almost familiar under your tracing fingers.
* [History DC 13] Make out the names -> clan_study_ok | clan_study_plain
* [Investigation DC 13] Compare the stone to the one on Yorgrim's back -> clan_study_ok | clan_study_plain
* Leave it -> END

~ clan_study_ok
set mm_clan_names_learned
Narrator: They are orc names, all of the Shadestone bloodline. Every last one matches a name carved into the heavy slab chained to Yorgrim's back.
-> END

~ clan_study_plain
Narrator: The mossy grooves blur beneath your thumb, refusing to yield their meaning.
-> END

~ lantern_statue
Narrator: A cowled figure of grey stone holds an iron lantern out over the flagstones. Down at its plinth, fresh letters are slowly scratching themselves into the rock.
-> END
